  "strategy": "5. Approach and rationale:\nWe will create a step function with non-negative coefficients that minimizes a score function defined as 2*n*max(convolve(a,a))/sum(a)^2. \nThe central idea is to find a sequence where the convolution does not have too large peaks and the sum is adjusted appropriately.\nTo address the previous program's score of 1.514229, we will use a more deterministic optimization method that minimizes the maximum of the convolution directly.\nThis will require balancing the length n and the sequence shape to keep the score low.\nOur algorithm will start from a flat spectrum sequence (like the alt_proj initialization) but then further refine it with gradient-based optimization.\nWe'll also consider dynamically adjusting the length during the search to potentially find better results.\n\n6. Mathematical specification:\nWe solve two optimization goals:\n1) The coefficients a of length n should be non-negative.\n2) The sum of the coefficients should be finite (at least 0.01) to prevent division by zero.\nThe evaluation function is:\nscore = 2*n * (max of convolution of a with itself) / (sum of a)^2\nThe optimization is done by gradient descent, but we must handle the fact that the objective is not differentiable at the maximum value in the convolution.\nWe approximate the gradient by taking the direction from the simplex projection and using it to update the coefficients.\n\n7. Implementation procedure:\nWe use a smooth convergence algorithm involving:\n- Initialization with a flat spectrum sequence using the alt_proj method.\n- Optimization loop with adaptive learning rate and momentum to handle numerical issues.\n- Dynamic length adjustment within time limits to escape local minima.\n- Validation of the best sequence found so far.\nSupporting functions include:\nfast_convolve(a): Fast convolution via FFT.\nproject_simplex(v): Projects to the simplex (non-negative and sum 1).\nalternate_scale(a): Scales the sequence to alternate magnitudes for better spectrum.\ncandidate_pmha(a): Function to evaluate a candidate sequence.\nfind_opt_path(a): Main optimization function.\n\n8. Search choices and variations:\nWe explore multiple length configurations and initialization methods. Optimizations are scheduled dynamically, and the best solution is updated whenever a better one is found.\nThe algorithm uses adaptive learning rate and other hyperparameters to ensure efficiency.\n\n9. Compute budget and robustness:\nThe algorithm is designed to run within 1500 seconds. We use efficient helper functions and try expensive variations only when time permits.\nWe ensure non-negative sequences and finite evaluations.\n\n10. Validation and return contract:\nThe final candidate is validated by evaluating_sequence(). We keep the best sequence throughout optimization and print details for monitoring.\nThe program self-falls back to alt_proj if evaluations are invalid or infinite.",
